#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233383 :question/problem "What is the sum of $\\overset{\\to }{A}=⟨10,5⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{B}=⟨8,-2⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\overset{\\to }{A}+\\overset{\\to }{B},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{A}+\\overset{\\to }{B} & =⟨10,5⟩+⟨8,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨18,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233395 :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{U}=⟨1,3⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{V}=⟨6,3⟩$, then $\\overset{\\to }{U}+\\overset{\\to }{V}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{U}+\\overset{\\to }{V} & =⟨1,3⟩+⟨6,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+6,3+3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233442 :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{A}=⟨4,\\frac{1}{2}⟩$, then $-4\\overset{\\to }{A}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $-4\\overset{\\to }{A},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\overset{\\to }{A} & =-4\\cdot ⟨4,\\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(-4)\\cdot 4,(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-16,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233472 :question/problem "The antiderivative of $\\sqrt[3]{x}$ equals{{field-1}} $+\\,\\text{ }C$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\sqrt[3]{x}={x}^{1/3}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/3+1}}{(\\frac{1}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4/3}}{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4/3})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}{x}^{4/3-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233500 :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,1)$ for the curve $y\\operatorname{cos}(x)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $y\\operatorname{cos}(x)$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\operatorname{cos}(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}(x))+\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =0 \\\\ y(-\\operatorname{sin}(x))+\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y\\operatorname{sin}(x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}(x)}{\\operatorname{cos}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,1)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(0,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}(x)}{\\operatorname{cos}(x)}}_{(0,\\,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(0)}{\\operatorname{cos}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 0}{1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233501 :question/problem "The equation of a curve is $xy+{x}^{2}=1.$ The normal line at the point $(1,\\,0)$ intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(1,\\,0).$   Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{′},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy+{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y+2x & =0 \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-(2x+y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2x+y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,0)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(1,\\,0)} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{2x+y}{x}\\right)}_{(1,\\,0)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\cdot 1+0}{1} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is $$\n\\displaystyle {m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{-2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$ We can write the equation of the normal at the point $(1,\\,0)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-0) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}(0-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233502 :question/problem "![](images/dc/dc45107eb076c0dc199718def7bb3a34b73ad61ca00bda61e09472a9ea589c84.png)\n\nThe equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$  represents a circle. The tangent line at the point $(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ on the circle intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the tangent line at the point $(\\sqrt{2},\\,\\sqrt{2}).$ \n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2x+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2x}{2y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\sqrt{2},\\,\\sqrt{2})$  is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(\\sqrt{2},\\,\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{x}{y}\\right)}_{(\\sqrt{2},\\,\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the tangent at the point $(\\sqrt{2},\\,\\sqrt{2})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-\\sqrt{2}) & =-1(x-\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ y & =-(x-\\sqrt{2})+\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this tangent line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(x-\\sqrt{2})+\\sqrt{2} \\\\ y & =-(0-\\sqrt{2})+\\sqrt{2} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2} \\\\ y & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,2\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233539 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+t,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}-2,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2}+t \\\\  & ={(t+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(t+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${(t+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\ge 0,$ we can conclude that $x\\ge -\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that ${t}^{2}\\ge 0.$ Therefore, we can conclude that $y\\ge -2.$\n\nSo, the ranges are $x\\ge -\\frac{1}{4}$ and $y\\ge -2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233560 :question/problem "The coefficients $a$ and $b$ also satisfy the equation $20b+400a=7\\,200.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$b\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}b &=480-10a \\\\ 20b+400a &=7\\,200\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=480-10a\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $b$ in the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20b+400a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 20(480-10a)+400a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 9\\,600-200a+400a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 9\\,600+200a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 200a & =-2\\,400 \\\\ a & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-12$ into $b=480-10a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =480-10(-12) \\\\ b & =600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-12,\\,b=600.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233567 :question/problem "The coefficients $a$ and $b$ also satisfy the equation $30b+900a=6\\,000.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$b\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}b &=240-20a \\\\ 30b+900a &=6\\,000\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=240-20a\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $b$ in the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30b+900a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 30(240-20a)+900a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 7\\,200-600a+900a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 7\\,200+300a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 300a & =-1\\,200 \\\\ a & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-4$ into $b=240-20a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =240-20(-4) \\\\ b & =320\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-4,\\,b=320.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233574 :question/problem "The coefficients $a$ and $b$ also satisfy the equation $70a+9b=600.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$b\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}b &=75-5a \\\\ 70a+9b &=600\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=75-5a\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $b$ in the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}70a+9b & =600 \\\\ 70a+9(75-5a) & =600 \\\\ 70a+675-45a & =600 \\\\ 25a+675 & =600 \\\\ 25a & =-75 \\\\ a & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-3$ into $b=75-5a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =75-5(-3) \\\\ b & =90\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-3,\\,b=90.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233594 :question/problem "Let $a=⟨5,9⟩.$ Find $|\\,a\\,|.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\,| & =\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(5)^{2}+(9)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+81} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{106}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233595 :question/problem "if $a=⟨-4,8⟩$, then  what is the magnitude of  $a?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\,| & =\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233596 :question/problem "![](images/1a/1a250499768a91e3e225a05d141e42d7ed89168ba82c5560777f64b8b29fac4a.png)\n\nLet $C(-1,3)$ and $D(1,-2).$ Find $|\\overset{\\to }{CD}|.$" :question/worked-solution "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OC}=c=-i+3j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OD}=d=i-2j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{CD}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CD} & =\\overset{\\to }{OD}-\\overset{\\to }{OC} \\\\  & =d-c \\\\  & =(i-2j)-(-i+3j) \\\\  & =2i-5j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{CD}| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233598 :question/problem "![](images/1e/1e3e9c336bf85cf0f19b2326f12c5fd986fee5b0aae65b8d79257ea234efee13.png)\n\nPoints $P$ and $Q$ are shown in the picture above. Find $|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}|.$" :question/worked-solution "From the picture, we obtain $P(1,1)$ and $Q(2,3).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OP}=p=i+j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OQ}=q=2i+3j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =q-p \\\\  & =(2i+3j)-(i+j) \\\\  & =i+2j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233645 :question/problem "Given that $\\operatorname{log}(y)-{x}^{2}=3,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}(y)-{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{log}(y)-{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{log}(y))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{ln}(10)}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)-2x & =0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}(10)}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x)(y\\operatorname{ln}(10)) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\operatorname{ln}(10)\\,xy\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233647 :question/problem "Given that ${y}^{2}-4x+y=2,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4x+y & =2 \\\\ {y}^{2}+y & =2+4x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}+y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2+4x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(2y+1) & =4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4}{2y+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233657 :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed sine function with a minimum value of $0$ and a maximum value of $4.$" :question/worked-solution "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half. \n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{\\text{min}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2} & =\\frac{0+4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233658 :question/problem "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed cosine function with a minimum value of $-14$ and a maximum value of $-4.$" :question/worked-solution "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half. \n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{\\text{min}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2} & =\\frac{-14+(-4)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-18}{2} \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=-9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233683 :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}(3x)+1.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\operatorname{sin}x$ is\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}(3x)\\le 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-2$ and then adding $1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -1\\le \\operatorname{sin}(3x)\\le 1 \\\\  & 2\\ge -2\\operatorname{sin}(2x)\\ge -2 \\\\  & 3\\ge -2\\operatorname{sin}(2x)+1\\ge -1 \\\\  & -1\\le f(x)\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $f(x)\\in [-1,3].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233713 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$\n\nThe range of $x$ is \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x\\le $\n{{field-3}}.\n\n \n\nThe range of $y$ is \n{{field-4}}\n$\\le y$\n{{field-5}}{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -4 & \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 4 \\\\ -4 & \\le x\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-4\\le x\\le 4.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -2 & \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 2 \\\\ -2 & \\le y\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-2\\le y\\le 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233751 :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Note that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}={x}^{-4}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-4$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4+1}}{-4+1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{-3}}{3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233752 :question/problem "Calculate the integral of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}$" :question/worked-solution "Note that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{5/2}}={x}^{-5/2}.\n$$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}} & =\\int {x}^{-5/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-5/2+1}}{-\\frac{5}{2}+1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{-3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{3/2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233753 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-10}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-10}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1+1}}{1+1}{|}_{-10}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-10}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(-10)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2-50 \\\\  & =-48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233754 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/2+1}}{-\\frac{1}{2}+1}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2{x}^{1/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2({4}^{1/2}-{1}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =2(\\sqrt{4}-1) \\\\  & =2(2-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233774 :question/problem "Differentiate ${3}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}(x).$\n\n$({3}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}(x))^{'}=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,{3}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={3}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({3}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}(x))^{'} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =({3}^{x})^{'}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(x)+{3}^{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}(x))^{'} \\\\  & =({3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(3))(\\operatorname{sin}(x))+({3}^{x})(\\operatorname{cos}(x)) \\\\  & ={3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(3)\\operatorname{sin}(x)+{3}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233776 :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=3{x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{tan}x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={sec}^{2}x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2})({sec}^{2}x)+(\\operatorname{tan}x)(6x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{sec}^{2}x+6x\\operatorname{tan}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi $ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=\\pi } & =3{x}^{2}{sec}^{2}x+6x\\operatorname{tan}x{|}_{x=\\pi } \\\\  & =3(\\pi )^{2}{sec}^{2}(\\pi )+6(\\pi )\\operatorname{tan}(\\pi ) \\\\  & =3{\\pi }^{2}\\cdot 1+6\\pi \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3{\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $3{\\pi }^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233804 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{32}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{32}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{32}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{32}{x}^{-2/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-2/5+1}}{(-\\frac{2}{5}+1)}{|}_{1}^{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\,{x}^{3/5}{|}_{1}^{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\,{(\\sqrt[5]{x})}^{3}{|}_{1}^{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}({(\\sqrt[5]{32})}^{3}-{(\\sqrt[5]{1})}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}({2}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233808 :question/problem "Differentiate $(2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))\\operatorname{cos}(x)$\n\n$((2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))\\operatorname{cos}(x))^{'}=-{sec}^{2}(x)\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=2-\\operatorname{tan}(x)$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{cos}(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))\\operatorname{cos}(x))^{'} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =(2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))^{'}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}(x)+(2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}(x))^{'} \\\\  & =(-{sec}^{2}(x))(\\operatorname{cos}(x))+(2-\\operatorname{tan}(x))(-\\operatorname{sin}(x)) \\\\  & =-{sec}^{2}(x)\\operatorname{cos}(x)+(\\operatorname{tan}(x)-2)\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233809 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={5}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}(x),$ find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,{5}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={5}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & ={({5}^{x})}^{'}\\operatorname{sin}(x)+{5}^{x}{(\\operatorname{sin}(x))}^{'} \\\\  & =({5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(5))\\operatorname{sin}(x)+{5}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}(x)) \\\\  & ={5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(5)\\operatorname{sin}(x)+{5}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233814 :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(x-1)\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x-1$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{2}{2x+1}+\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-1)}{2x+1}+\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & =\\frac{2(0-1)}{2(0)+1}+\\operatorname{ln}(2(0)+1) \\\\  & =(-2)+\\operatorname{ln}(1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233815 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{2x},$ find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=2x{e}^{2x}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)={e}^{2x}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{'}{e}^{2x}+{x}^{2}({e}^{2x})^{'} \\\\  & =(2x)({e}^{2x})+({x}^{2})(2{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2x}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233831 :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y=1-{t}^{2},\\,1\\le t\\le 6.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-1}} | $\\frac{51}{2}$ | $62$ | $\\frac{245}{2}$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-3$ | {{field-3}} | $-15$ | $-24$ | $-35$ |" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $x(2),$ we substitute $t=2$ into $x={t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}t.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(2)=(2)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}(2)=8-1=7.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(3),$ we substitute $t=3$ into $y=1-{t}^{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(3)=1-(3)^{2}=1-9=-8.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $x(6),$ we substitute $t=6$ into $x={t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}t.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(6)=(6)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}(6)=216-3=213.\n$$\n\nThen the table is \n\n| **$t$** | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $7$ | $\\frac{51}{2}$ | $62$ | $\\frac{245}{2}$ | $213$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-8$ | $-15$ | $-24$ | $-35$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233838 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -3,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$\n\nThe range of $x$ is \n{{field-1}}\n$\\le x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}.\n\nThe range of $y$ is \n{{field-4}}{{field-5}}\n$y\\le $\n{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -5 & \\le 5\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 5 \\\\ -5-3 & \\le 5\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -3\\le 5-3 \\\\ -8 & \\le x\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-8\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -7 & \\le 7\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 7 \\\\ -7+1 & \\le 7\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +1\\le 7+1 \\\\ -6 & \\le y\\le 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-6\\le y\\le 8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233920 :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(2,\\,7)$ for the curve $\\sqrt{x+7}+\\sqrt{y+2}=6.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x+7}+\\sqrt{y+2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((x+7)^{1/2}+(y+2)^{1/2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(x+7)^{-1/2}+\\frac{1}{2}(y+2)^{-1/2}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(y+2)^{-1/2}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x+7)^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y+2}}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+7}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{y+2}}{\\sqrt{x+7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(2,\\,7)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,\\,7)} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{y+2}}{\\sqrt{x+7}}\\right)|}_{(2,\\,7)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{7+2}}{\\sqrt{2+7}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{9}}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233921 :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(1,4)$ for the curve ${x}^{3}y=4x.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{3}y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}y & =4x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x) \\\\ 3{x}^{2}y+{x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4 \\\\ {x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4-3{x}^{2}y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4-3{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(1,4)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,4)} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{4-3{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}}\\right)|}_{(1,4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-3(1)^{2}(4)}{(1)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-8)}{1} \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233931 :question/problem "The equation of a curve is $8y={x}^{2}+4x.$ The normal line at the point $(4,4)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are $($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(4,4).$ Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{'},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8y & ={x}^{2}+4x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+4x) \\\\ 8\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x+4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x+4}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x+2}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(4,4)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(4,\\,4)} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{x+2}{4}\\right)|}_{(4,\\,4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the normal line at the point $(4,4)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{'}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-4) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{20}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $x$-axis. At the point $P,$ the $y$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $y=0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{20}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}x & =\\frac{20}{3} \\\\ x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(10,\\,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233949 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{2+1}}{2+1}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{3} \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233950 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{-4+1}}{-4+1}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3}}{-3}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{3}}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {3}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {1}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{81}-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233951 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{-2+1}}{-2+1}{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{-1}{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{(-1)}-\\frac{1}{(-2)}) \\\\  & =-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
